# GraphQL-focused wirefilter expressions for graphql-waf
# -------------------------------------------------------
# The proxy loads a single `--wirefilter`/`waf.wirefilter` expression, so treat this file as a
# menu: copy ONE expression (no `#` lines) into your config, or combine multiple conditions with
# `&&`/`||` into a single expression yourself.
#
# Note: the wirefilter scheme (see `src/proxy.rs::build_scheme`) only exposes method, host,
# user-agent, content-type, path, query string, and a few headers -- it has no visibility into
# the request body. Body/query-shape enforcement (introspection, depth, aliases, cost, batching)
# is handled by the dedicated GraphQL security layer (`--graphql-security` and the
# `--graphql-max-*` flags), not by wirefilter. These expressions are for header/metadata-level
# defense-in-depth on top of that, not a replacement for it.
#
# In block mode, a true expression counts as a WAF match (request is blocked).

# Only enforce extra scrutiny on GraphQL POSTs (pair with ModSecurity/graphql-security for
# body-level checks). Same as the general example in wirefilter.example.
http.method == "POST" && http.request.uri.path contains "graphql"

# Reject GraphQL POSTs with a content-type that isn't one of the standard GraphQL transports.
# Naive scanners/scripts often send `text/plain` or omit content-type entirely.
# http.request.uri.path contains "graphql" && http.method == "POST" && \
#   not (http.content_type contains "application/json" || http.content_type contains "application/graphql")

# Flag empty-user-agent requests to the GraphQL endpoint -- a common signal for scripted
# scanners/bots that don't bother setting a UA at all.
# http.request.uri.path contains "graphql" && http.user_agent == ""

# Known scanner/tooling user-agent substrings hitting the GraphQL endpoint directly (adjust the
# list to whatever you've observed in your own logs; this is illustrative, not exhaustive).
# http.request.uri.path contains "graphql" && \
#   (http.user_agent contains "sqlmap" || http.user_agent contains "graphql-cop" || http.user_agent contains "InQL")

# Block GraphiQL playground access for anything that arrived via a proxy/load balancer (i.e.
# has an X-Forwarded-For value at all) as a rough "internal traffic only" approximation --
# direct/internal callers typically won't have this header set. Not a substitute for real
# network-level access control (the scheme has no CIDR/IP-range matching on this Bytes field).
# http.request.uri.path == "/graphiql" && http.request.headers.x_forwarded_for != ""
